					'tasks' => array(
						'test' => array(
							array('question' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] In xv6, the interrupt handler ideintr uses the lock idelock to ensure that it executes atomically. For example, if processor 0 runs iderw and processor 1 runs the interrupt han­ dler ideintr, then the lock idelock ensures that their critical sections are appropriately ordered. Why is it important that acquire(&idelock) also turns off interrupts, in addition to marking the lock as acquired?', 'mode' => 'text', 'xp' => 20, 'hint' => 'Problème d’examen du MIT : rédige ta réponse, puis la correction officielle du MIT s’affiche.', 'explanation' => 'Corrige officiel du MIT : Suppose an IDE interrupt occurred on processor 0 while iderw held the idelock on processor 0. If acquire did not disable interrupts, this interrupt would be serviced immediately, resulting in a call to ideintr, which would try to acquire the idelock. This would panic because the lock is already held on processor 0. (If acquire did not check for this situation, it would simply deadlock, waiting for the lock to be released while blocking the very processor holding the lock.)

[Correction officielle MIT : [MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] In xv6, the interrupt handler ideintr uses the lock idelock to ensure that it executes atomically. For example, if processor 0 runs iderw and processor 1 runs the interrupt han­ dler ideintr, then the lock idelock ensures that their critical sections are appropriately ordered. Why is it important that acquire(&idelock) also turns off interrupts, in addition to marking the lock as acquired?]', 'source' => 'MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.828 Operating System Engineering, Fall 2012 — examen et corrigé officiel (MIT EECS)', 'licence' => 'CC BY-NC-SA 4.0', 'answer' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] In xv6, the interrupt handler ideintr uses the lock idelock to ensure that it executes atomically. For example, if processor 0 runs iderw and processor 1 runs the interrupt han­ dler ideintr, then the lock idelock ensures that their critical sections are appropriately ordered. Why is it important that acquire(&idelock) also turns off interrupts, in addition to marking the lock as acquired?', '_recupere_le' => '2026-11-04T19:55:22.760631+00:00Z', '_strategie' => 'mode_texte_ouvert', 'answers' => array('Corrige officiel du MIT : Suppose an IDE interrupt occurred on processor 0 while iderw held the idelock on processor 0. If acquire did not disable interrupts, this interrupt would be serviced immediat')),
							array('question' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] You have a machine with 4 GB of DRAM (and 32-bit addresses). What new value should you give KERNBASE to allow a single environment to allocate and address the largest number of distinct user memory pages possible? It’s okay to be off by a few dozen megabytes. Explain why a KERNBASE higher or lower than your', 'mode' => 'choice', 'xp' => 20, 'hint' => 'Problème d’examen du MIT : rédige ta réponse, puis la correction officielle du MIT s’affiche.', 'explanation' => 'Corrige officiel du MIT : About 0x80000000 (2 gigabytes). A higher KERNBASE would reduce the available physi­ cal memory; JOS can only use physical pages that it can access above KERNBASE. A lower KERN­ BASE would reduce user virtual address space available to the environment.', 'source' => 'MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.828 Operating System Engineering, Fall 2012 — examen et corrigé officiel (MIT EECS)', 'licence' => 'CC BY-NC-SA 4.0', 'choices' => array('Pas would reduce the maximum allocatable environment size.', 'L\'inverse de would reduce the maximum allocatable environment size.', 'would reduce the maximum allocatable environment size.', 'Impossible à déterminer'), 'correct' => 2, 'answer' => 'would reduce the maximum allocatable environment size.', '_recupere_le' => '2026-11-04T19:55:22.760792+00:00Z', '_strategie' => 'separation_enum_solution'),
