					'tasks' => array(
						'test' => array(
							array('question' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Explain why it is safe to keep sensitive kernel data structures mapped at 0xF0000000 while executing user-level code, which should not be able to access those kernel data structures.', 'mode' => 'text', 'xp' => 20, 'hint' => 'Problème d’examen du MIT : rédige ta réponse, puis la correction officielle du MIT s’affiche.', 'explanation' => 'Corrige officiel du MIT : JOS only sets the PTE U bit in the PTEs of pages that the current environment is allowed to use, and in particular, does not set PTE U for kernel memory mapped above 0xF0000000. The x86 paging hardware doesn\'t allow access to pages without PTE U when CPL is 3.

[Correction officielle MIT : [MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Explain why it is safe to keep sensitive kernel data structures mapped at 0xF0000000 while executing user-level code, which should not be able to access those kernel data structures.]', 'source' => 'MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.828 Operating System Engineering, Fall 2012 — examen et corrigé officiel (MIT EECS)', 'licence' => 'CC BY-NC-SA 4.0', 'answer' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Explain why it is safe to keep sensitive kernel data structures mapped at 0xF0000000 while executing user-level code, which should not be able to access those kernel data structures.', '_recupere_le' => '2026-11-04T19:55:22.761202+00:00Z', '_strategie' => 'mode_texte_ouvert', 'answers' => array('Corrige officiel du MIT : JOS only sets the PTE U bit in the PTEs of pages that the current environment is allowed to use, and in particular, does not set PTE U for kernel memory mapped above 0xF00000')),
							array('question' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Assume xv6 does no repair of a ﬁle system after a reboot (it has no fsck). Alyssa starts by thinking about a single aspect of consistency: preventing the same block from be­ ing referenced by two different ﬁles; she will worry about other aspects later. Which writes during echo ab > x above must be synchronous to ensure this does not happen? Provide a brief explana­ tion.', 'mode' => 'text', 'xp' => 20, 'hint' => 'Problème d’examen du MIT : rédige ta réponse, puis la correction officielle du MIT s’affiche.', 'explanation' => 'Corrige officiel du MIT : Only the write of sector 28, which marks the file data block in-use, must be synchronous. This ensures that the block allocation write cannot occur after the first write to the inode, which creates the reference to the block. If the inode write were to happen first, followed by a crash, then after the system rebooted and read the bitmap back in, it could allocate that block for another purpose, even though it\'s actually in use. The remaining writes simply update file data and the file length. Alyssa goes on to wonder how to extend xv6 to support named pipes. A named pipe allows the following shell script: $ mkfifo mypipe $ cat file > mypipe & $ wc < mypipe $ rm mypipe This shell code behaves much like cat file | wc, except that the pipe is given a regular file name that is stored in the file system, even though the contents of the pipe are not. Just like a regular pipe, the kernel passes all data from the writer to the reader internally, via in-memory buffers (without writing to the disk). Unlike a regular pipe, a named pipe appears in the file system, as a "special file." As a result, the processes communicating over the pipe don\'t have to have a parent-child relationship, since they can open the pipe by its file name (as in the example).

[Correction officielle MIT : [MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Assume xv6 does no repair of a ﬁle system after a reboot (it has no fsck). Alyssa starts by thinking about a single aspect of consistency: preventing the same block from be­ ing referenced by two different ﬁles; she will worry about other aspects later. Which writes during echo ab > x above must be synchronous to ensure this does not happen? Provide a brief explana­ tion.]', 'source' => 'MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.828 Operating System Engineering, Fall 2012 — examen et corrigé officiel (MIT EECS)', 'licence' => 'CC BY-NC-SA 4.0', 'answer' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Assume xv6 does no repair of a ﬁle system after a reboot (it has no fsck). Alyssa starts by thinking about a single aspect of consistency: preventing the same block from be­ ing referenced by two different ﬁles; she will worry about other aspects later. Which writes during echo ab > x above must be synchronous to ensure this does not happen? Provide a brief explana­ tion.', '_recupere_le' => '2026-11-04T19:55:22.761340+00:00Z', '_strategie' => 'mode_texte_ouvert', 'answers' => array('Corrige officiel du MIT : Only the write of sector 28, which marks the file data block in-use, must be synchronous. This ensures that the block allocation write cannot occur after the first write to t')),
