				'les-fondamentaux-du-reseau' => array(
					'title' => 'les-fondamentaux-du-reseau',
					'tasks' => array(
						'test' => array(
							array('question' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Describe two situations in which a power failure during a system call could leave different parts of the xv6 ﬁle system metadata inconsistent with each other. By “metadata” we mean the ﬁle system’s on-disk data structures: the superblock, inodes, indirect blocks, the block in-use bitmap, and directory contents. A power failure halts the CPU and stops the disk after completion of the current sector read/write request (if any). Be sure to indicate the line number after which you assume the power failure occurs for each of the two situations, and the nature of the resulting inconsistencies.', 'mode' => 'text', 'xp' => 20, 'hint' => 'Problème d’examen du MIT : rédige ta réponse, puis la correction officielle du MIT s’affiche.', 'explanation' => 'Corrige officiel du MIT : There are many possibilities. Some lead to a block or inode not being marked free, but also not being used. Some lead to user-visible incorrectness. Suppose a process is writing to a file, and bmap() decides it must allocate a new block to hold the data (line 4019), and calls balloc(). If a crash were to occur after line 3718 in balloc(), the block would be marked as in-use in the on-disk bitmap, but no inode would refer to it. This would not cause any immediate damage if the file system were to be used after a reboot, but it would decrease the amount of usable space in the file system. As a second example, suppose a process is creating a new directory with the mkdir() system call, which calls create(), and the system crashed after line 4836. The new directory would be visible in its parent directory (due to the dirlink() at line 4831) but the new directory would not have "." or ".." entries. If the system rebooted and a user process looked at the new directory it would look odd.

[Correction officielle MIT : [MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Describe two situations in which a power failure during a system call could leave different parts of the xv6 ﬁle system metadata inconsistent with each other. By “metadata” we mean the ﬁle system’s on-disk data structures: the superblock, inodes, indirect blocks, the block in-use bitmap, and directory contents. A power failure halts the CPU and stops the disk after completion of the current sector read/write request (if any). Be sure to indicate the line number after which you assume the power failure occurs for each of the two situations, and the nature of the resulting inconsistencies.]', 'source' => 'MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.828 Operating System Engineering, Fall 2012 — examen et corrigé officiel (MIT EECS)', 'licence' => 'CC BY-NC-SA 4.0', 'answer' => '[MIT 6.828 — problème d’examen, en anglais] Describe two situations in which a power failure during a system call could leave different parts of the xv6 ﬁle system metadata inconsistent with each other. By “metadata” we mean the ﬁle system’s on-disk data structures: the superblock, inodes, indirect blocks, the block in-use bitmap, and directory contents. A power failure halts the CPU and stops the disk after completion of the current sector read/write request (if any). Be sure to indicate the line number after which you assume the power failure occurs for each of the two situations, and the nature of the resulting inconsistencies.', '_recupere_le' => '2026-11-04T19:55:22.761538+00:00Z', '_strategie' => 'mode_texte_ouvert', 'answers' => array('Corrige officiel du MIT : There are many possibilities. Some lead to a block or inode not being marked free, but also not being used. Some lead to user-visible incorrectness. Suppose a process is writ')),
